"Taiwan is a rainy island. According to the Water Resources Agency, it gets about 2,500 millimeters of rain in an average year, 2.6 times the world average. Yet the Water Resources Agency warns that Taiwan's water is hard to store and use. How can a place with so much rain run short?": "19bdc3810de5",
"Taiwan is a rainy island. According to the Water Resources Agency, it gets about 2,500 millimeters of rain in an average year, 2.6 times the world average. Yet the Water Resources Agency warns that Taiwan's water is hard to store and use. How can a place with so much rain run short? The first reason is timing. About 78 percent of the year's rain falls from May to October, during the plum rains and the typhoon season. In the south the difference is even bigger: in Kaohsiung, about 91 out of every 100 millimeters fall in those six months. From November to April, it hardly rains there at all. The second reason is the shape of the land. As Lesson 9 showed, Taiwan's mountains are high and close to the sea, so its rivers are short and steep. The Water Resources Agency explains that after the rain flows into the rivers, most of it runs straight into the sea. Rain that falls on the east side can reach the sea in just a few dozen kilometers. On the 3D model, every drop of rain follows the land downhill. The main mountain ridge is a divide: rain on one side flows west, and rain on the other side flows east. West of the ridge, the rivers are longer. The Zhuoshui River, Taiwan's longest, runs 186.6 kilometers, while the Xiuguluan River on the east coast is about 81 kilometers long. The third reason is people. Taiwan is crowded, as Lesson 8 showed. The Water Resources Agency works out that each person's share of the rain is about 4,000 tons a year, less than one-fifth of the world average. Rain that runs into the sea in a few hours cannot be shared later. So Taiwan stores water. At the end of 2025 there were 95 reservoirs, 66 of them on the main island. But their measured useful storage is only about three-quarters of what they were designed to hold. Saving water, catching rain, and using water more than once all help a rainy island get through the dry season.": "6dbe73f6f393",
